    id: 'll1-the-perfect-yahweh-expects',
    stories: [{"kind":"parable","tone":"light","title":"The Baker's Empty Shelves","body":"Naomi ran what everyone agreed was the finest bakery in town, and yet no cake had ever once reached her display case. A hair too much lean on the left, a single stubborn bubble under the glaze, a crumb of a shadow where the frosting should have gone glassy, and into the bin it went. Her shelves stayed spotless and bare while the warm, maddening smell of ruined perfection drifted all the way down the street, teasing every passerby. Folks stopped asking to buy. They just came to smell what they could not have. One gray morning a small girl pressed her nose flat against the glass, breath fogging it, and asked why on earth a bakery would sell nothing at all. \"Because none of them are perfect yet,\" Naomi said, not unkindly, already reaching for the next batch to inspect. The girl only shrugged, unbothered by grown-up logic. \"My grandma's cakes lean sideways. The whole side sinks in, even. And everybody eats them and hugs her and asks for more.\" Naomi opened her mouth to explain the difference, then found she had none. She looked a long, quiet moment at her flawless, empty shelves, at years of good bread thrown away in the name of a standard nobody had ever actually asked her to meet. Slowly she reached back into the bin, lifted out a cake that tilted just slightly to one side, dusted it, and set it in the window where the light could reach it. It sold in under a minute, to a man who wept a little at the taste. And Naomi, at long last, let herself keep baking, learning that whole and given and shared was a truer kind of finished than flawless and hidden had ever been.","verse":"Philippians 3:12"},{"kind":"parable","tone":"solemn","title":"The Servant Who Could Not Rest","body":"Every night, long after the household had gone still and dark, Eli rose and walked the halls again. He tested each latch he had already tested, pressed each bolt he had already thrown, and rehearsed in a low whisper the morning he was certain would come, the morning he would at last be found wanting and turned out. He grew thin under the weight of that dread. His hands trembled at their work. He could not taste his food, could not hold a full night's sleep, because sleep felt like the gate through which failure would slip in unnoticed. The dread had become his master, and it was a cruel one, promising that one forgotten lock would undo everything and prove what he already believed of himself. One evening the head of the house, unable to sleep himself, found the servant frozen at a door already bolted, shaking, his knuckles white against the frame. The old man asked, gently, what could possibly frighten a faithful man so. \"That I will fail you,\" Eli whispered, \"and be cast out into the cold.\" The old man did not scold him, did not list his duties, did not remind him of the standard. Instead he lowered himself down to the cold stone floor, slowly, beside his servant, and sat. \"You were mine,\" he said, \"before you ever drew a single bolt. I did not take you in for your locks, and I will not put you out for one. Sit here with me now. You may stop being afraid.\" And slowly, the way warmth thaws a hand left too long in the snow, aching first and then easing, the fear went out of Eli. That night, held by love instead of driven by terror, he slept, and it was deep, and it was the first true rest he had known in years.","verse":"1 John 4:18"}],
