    id: 'll34-the-tower-the-race-and-the-sovereign',
    stories: [{"kind":"parable","tone":"light","title":"The Bunker in the Basement","body":"Ever since the headlines started using words like \"superintelligence,\" Gerald had not slept right. He read every article twice, watched the frightening videos until two in the morning, and began, quietly, to prepare. He filled the basement with canned beans. He bought a radio that cranked. He unplugged the smart speaker and glared at the doorbell camera as though it were plotting against him. \"You can't be too careful,\" he told his neighbor Pete over the fence, clutching a printout of predictions for the year 2040. Pete, who was watering his tomatoes, considered the printout. \"That's a lot of beans,\" he said. \"The machines are going to run everything!\" \"Maybe,\" said Pete. \"And maybe the fellow who wrote that is guessing. He put a big number on it, but a big number on a guess is still a guess.\" He turned off the hose. \"Gerald, you look like a man who hasn't slept.\" \"I haven't.\" \"You know what I did today? I taught my granddaughter to plant seeds, I called my brother, and I paid my tithe. Same things I'll do in 2040, Lord willing, whatever the robots are up to.\" He picked a ripe tomato and passed it over the fence. \"The One who runs the times was on the throne long before the computers showed up. He didn't hand in His resignation.\" Gerald looked at the tomato in his hand. It was warm from the sun and entirely real, which was more than he could say for the year 2040. He ate the tomato. He slept that night — eight full hours. The beans, it turned out, kept just fine without his worrying over them. Fear was never the assignment. A sound mind was.","verse":"2 Timothy 1:7"},{"kind":"parable","tone":"solemn","title":"Two Builders and a Name","body":"On the edge of a growing city, two builders broke ground the same spring. The first, Halgar, wanted his name on the skyline. He would raise the tallest tower the plain had ever seen — higher than the cathedral, higher than the granaries, a monument that would make the whole valley speak his name for a hundred years. He hired every mason he could find and drove them through the dark. When an old surveyor warned that the marsh beneath the site would not hold such weight, Halgar laughed. \"I have gold enough to buy more stone. Nothing will stop me now.\" The second builder, Tomas, raised only a modest house and a well. Before he laid a single stone he spent three days testing the ground, and he set his foundation on the rock shelf at the plain's edge, though it meant a smaller house than he had hoped. Neighbors thought him timid. Halgar thought him a fool. The tower rose astonishingly fast — five floors, then eight, then twelve, a wonder that drew crowds. And then, in the autumn rains, the marsh did what marshes do. The great weight pressed down, the ground sighed, and in a single grey dawn the tower leaned, cracked, and came down into the mud with a sound like the end of the world. Halgar stood in the wreckage of his name. Tomas's small house kept its lamp lit through the same rains, and travelers sheltered there. A man may build fast, and high, and for his own name. But when the Lord is not in the building, the builder labors for the mud — and the marsh keeps its own patient accounting.","verse":"Psalms 127:1"}],
    // Captured 2026-07-17 from a teaching Darrell brought into the app -- an
