    title: 'The Tower, the Race, and the Sovereign — Discernment in the Age of AI',
    anchor: { ref: 'Genesis 11:4-6; Daniel 2:21; Psalm 127:1', theme: 'Let us build us a city and a tower... and let us make us a name... now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do. He changeth the times and the seasons. Except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain. Build, and be watchful -- but trust the One who holds the times, not the tower.' },
    bigIdea: 'A former AI researcher, Daniel Kokotajlo, gives a sobering account of where artificial intelligence is heading: systems that increasingly improve THEMSELVES (an AI running the research that trains a better AI -- a loop he warns could become an "intelligence explosion"), built by a handful of labs locked in a high-stakes RACE, behind closed doors, with -- in his forecast -- a serious chance of catastrophe if it is not managed. Take the facts soberly and the forecasts as forecasts: the rapid advance and the real tension between safety and the race are established; the exact odds and timelines are his estimates, not settled fact. But hear how ancient the SHAPE of this is. Humanity has built a self-amplifying tower before, to "make us a name," needing no one -- and God’s word over Babel reads almost eerily onto a self-improving machine: "now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do" (Genesis 11:4-6). The believer’s frame is not panic and not worship of the machine, but three steadying truths. First, GOD is sovereign over history -- "he changeth the times and the seasons: he removeth kings, and setteth up kings" (Daniel 2:21); "He that sitteth in the heavens shall laugh" (Psalm 2:4) -- so "God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind" (2 Timothy 1:7). Second, no human should be trusted with unchecked power -- "put not your trust in princes... in whom there is no help" (Psalm 146:3) -- and we judge by FRUITS, "by their fruits ye shall know them" (Matthew 7:20), not by anyone’s press release. Third, "except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain that build it" (Psalm 127:1).',
    inApp: 'This week, meet the age of AI as a watchful, unafraid steward. FIRST, REFUSE BOTH PANIC AND WORSHIP: the machine is a tool, powerful but not a god; rest in the Sovereign who "changeth the times and the seasons" (Daniel 2:21) -- "the spirit of... a sound mind," not fear (2 Timothy 1:7). SECOND, PROVE WHAT YOU ARE TOLD: from any lab, any hype, any doom -- "prove all things; hold fast that which is good" (1 Thessalonians 5:21); "try the spirits" (1 John 4:1); judge by FRUITS, not slogans (Matthew 7:20). THIRD, PRIZE WISDOM OVER RAW POWER: "knowledge puffeth up, but charity edifieth" (1 Corinthians 8:1); "the fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom" (Proverbs 9:10) -- the real "alignment problem" is the ancient one, power without the fear of God. FOURTH, STEWARD IT TOWARD PEOPLE: use these tools to serve real people and follow The Way, not to extract; and love your neighbor as jobs and lives are disrupted -- "let us do good unto all men" (Galatians 6:10). FIFTH, BUILD ON THE RIGHT FOUNDATION: whatever you make, remember "except the LORD build the house, they labour in vain" (Psalm 127:1).',
