    id: 'll41-truth-with-a-capital-t',
    stories: [{"kind":"parable","tone":"light","title":"The Weatherman He Wanted","body":"Del owned one necktie and one opinion he loved more than the tie: that his knees could forecast weather better than any satellite. When they ached, rain; when they didn't, sun. He planned the whole church picnic around them. The meteorologist on channel 7 warned of storms all week, but Del waved him off, the man was probably wrong, and besides, Del's knees felt fine. \"I go with what makes me feel confident,\" he told the deacons, thumping his chest. \"A cheerful heart's worth more than a radar dish.\" The deacons, who badly wanted the picnic to happen, agreed with enthusiasm. Nobody brought a tarp. The storm arrived at 12:15, precisely as channel 7 had said, and drove eighty Baptists under a single pavilion with the potato salad going soft in the rain. Del stood at the edge, water sheeting off his hat, insisting his knees had merely \"rounded down.\" His grandson, seven years old and soaked to the socks, looked up and asked the honest question: \"Grandpa, if your knees are wrong, why do you keep believing them?\" Del opened his mouth and found he had no answer, because he had never once believed his knees for being right. He had believed them for being his, and for telling him what he wanted to hear. A comfortable forecast had felt like a friend. It had also gotten everyone drenched. Del bought an umbrella the following week and, quietly, began listening to the man who was actually telling the truth, even when the truth called for a coat. What merely feels good cannot keep you dry. Only what is real can do that, and only the truth can set a stubborn man free.","verse":"John 8:32"},{"kind":"parable","tone":"solemn","title":"The Empty Chair","body":"When the Harmons lost their daughter, the casseroles came for a week and then stopped, and so did most of the visitors, because nobody knew what to say and everybody was afraid of saying it wrong. The house went quiet in the particular way a house goes quiet after a funeral. One evening old Pastor Oyelaran knocked, came in, and did the thing no one else had managed: he said nothing at all. He did not explain why it had happened. He did not reach for a reason, or a silver lining, or a tidy word to tie it off. He simply pulled a chair beside Mr. Harmon at the kitchen table, sat down in the ruin of it, and stayed. An hour passed. The refrigerator hummed. Somewhere a clock ticked toward nothing. When Mr. Harmon finally broke and wept into his hands, the old man did not tell him to stop. He wept too. Later, walking him to the door, Mr. Harmon managed the only words he had: \"You never told me it would be all right.\" \"No,\" the pastor said. \"I don't know when it will be. I only know you shouldn't sit in it alone.\" That was all. But it was the first night the Harmons slept. Others had offered them answers, and answers had bounced off the walls of their grief like coins tossed at a locked door. The old man had offered only his presence, and presence walked straight in. This is the thing the world cannot fathom about the Lord: He does not stand at a safe distance explaining the pain away. He draws near to the broken-hearted. He sits down in the ruin. And once, at a graveside, He wept.","verse":"John 11:35"}],
    // Captured 2026-07-18 -- Darrell fed a Steven Bartlett / Wes Huff conversation
