        "title": "Two Men and One Sermon",
        "body": "Marcus and Dell shared a pew for nine years. Same church, same seats, same preacher, and — that particular autumn — the same series on forgiveness, word for word, message after message. Marcus loved those Sundays. He would lean forward, nod hard, feel the sting in his chest when the teaching landed, and text his brother afterward: powerful today. Then he'd drive home replaying the argument he refused to end, the grudge he'd nursed for a decade against a cousin who wronged him, and by Monday the warm feeling had cooled to nothing. He never called the cousin. He never meant not to; the days just filled up with work and worry, and forgiveness stayed a subject he enjoyed, never a thing he did. Dell heard the identical words. But Dell went home and sat with them until they became uncomfortable, and then he did the uncomfortable thing — he wrote the letter, made the call, drove the two hours, and released the debt out loud. It cost him a hard, awkward afternoon. Nine years later the two men's lives did not resemble each other at all. Marcus was still a faithful attender, still moved by good preaching, still carrying the same quiet weight into every service, wondering vaguely why the sermons never seemed to change anything. Dell was lighter, and free, and had learned to release the next offense faster than the last. They had received the same seed. One let it lie on the surface and admired it; one let it break the ground and bear fruit. The difference was never the Word. It never is.",
        "verse": "James 1:22"
