    benefits: [
      'Dignity for a hidden grief: hair loss in menopause is named in the open, honored ("if a woman have long hair, it is a glory to her," 1 Corinthians 11:15) — and answered with stewardship instead of shame.',
      'Daniel\'s method for your health: test, measure, compare — "Prove thy servants, I beseech thee, ten days" (Daniel 1:12) — real numbers with your physician, never guessing and never despair.',
      'Freedom from food religion: eliminations are weighed as attributed counsel for a season and a body, never as righteousness — "the kingdom of God is not meat and drink" (Romans 14:17); "every creature of God is good... received with thanksgiving" (1 Timothy 4:4).',
      'A steward\'s calm instead of internet panic: "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good" (1 Thessalonians 5:21) — counsel tested, kept if good, released if not.',
      'The crown that cannot thin: "the hidden man of the heart... in the sight of God of great price" (1 Peter 3:4) — worth settled before any mirror is consulted.',
    ],
    levels: {
      child: 'Your body is like a wonderful house that God built just for you — the Bible says you are "fearfully and wonderfully made" (Psalm 139:14)! Inside your neck there is a tiny helper called the thyroid. Two doctors teach that it works like a gas pedal for your whole body — it tells all your parts how fast to go, even the little factories that grow your hair. If the tiny helper gets tired, the body saves its energy for the most important jobs first. So we take care of our bodies: we eat good food, and when something isn\'t working right, we go to the doctor and CHECK instead of guessing — just like Daniel asked for a ten-day test with vegetables and water to see what was true (Daniel 1:12-15). God gave you your body as a gift. Taking care of it is one way we say thank You.',
      teen: 'Real topic, especially for the women in your house: two doctors — Dr. Samantha Jean and Dr. Gerdie Jean-Smith — teach that the thyroid is the body\'s gas pedal, and that when it struggles (they report autoimmunity or missing nutrients as common causes), the body triages: survival systems first, and hair follicles — a "non-essential" — get sacrificed. That\'s one reason hair loss can ride with menopause. They report a gluten mechanism too: an enzyme your gut uses to process gluten also exists in thyroid tissue, so in prone people the immune system\'s attack can cross over and hit the thyroid. Their counsel: consider eliminating six inflammation drivers (gluten, dairy, soy, ultra-processed foods and seed oils, added sugars, non-iodized-only salt — they note iodine is raw material for thyroid hormone), ask a physician for the FULLER panel (Free T3/T4, thyroid antibodies, iodine, selenium, magnesium, vitamin D, iron with ferritin — they say TSH alone often isn\'t enough), and then supplement what testing actually shows. Now the Word\'s two rails, and hold both. Rail one: your body is a temple bought with a price (1 Corinthians 6:19-20) — tending it is worship, and Daniel\'s ten-day test (Daniel 1:12-15) is the Bible\'s own measure-don\'t-guess pattern; "Prove all things" (1 Thessalonians 5:21). Rail two: food rules are NEVER righteousness — "the kingdom of God is not meat and drink" (Romans 14:17), and "every creature of God is good" received with thanksgiving (1 Timothy 4:4) — so weigh the doctors\' counsel WITH a doctor for YOUR body, without fear and without judging anyone else\'s plate (Colossians 2:16). And the deepest rail: a woman\'s glory is real (1 Corinthians 11:15), but her worth was never hanging by a hair — "a woman that feareth the LORD, she shall be praised" (Proverbs 31:30).',
      senior: 'For the seasoned reader — and especially the mothers of the house walking menopause\'s real losses — this lesson carries a health teaching with the platform\'s full honesty discipline: every clinical claim below is the DOCTORS\' (Dr. Samantha Jean and Dr. Gerdie Jean-Smith), reported and attributed, to be weighed with your own physician — the house hands you counsel and the Word\'s frame, never a prescription. Their teaching: the thyroid regulates cellular pace body-wide; under autoimmune attack or nutritional gaps it slows, and hair — metabolically expensive, survival-optional — is sacrificed early. They report the gluten cross-reactivity mechanism (antibodies raised against gut transglutaminase finding the same enzyme in thyroid tissue), name six foods to eliminate for a season (gluten, dairy, soy, ultra-processed foods and seed oils, added sugars, and relying only on non-iodized salts — noting iodine is the raw material of thyroid hormone and that the sea-salt fashion has quietly starved it), urge the fuller laboratory panel beyond TSH (Free T3, Free T4, the antibody set TPO/TRab/TSI/TGAB, iodine, selenium, magnesium, vitamin D, iron with ferritin), and — once tested — report three supports: selenium (studies they cite show lowered antibodies; they warn mega-doses backfire), magnesium (required for TSH production and keratin), and vitamin D (low levels linked in their reporting to worse Hashimoto\'s symptoms; follicles carry vitamin D receptors; they suggest targeting 70 ng/mL rather than the bare minimum). The Word now sets this in its frame. The temple is His purchase: "ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body" (1 Corinthians 6:20); His wish over the beloved is health of body tracking health of soul (3 John 1:2); the Maker\'s handiwork is praised, not resented: "I will praise thee; for I am fearfully and wonderfully made" (Psalm 139:14). Daniel supplies the epistemology — a defined test, a defined term, an observed comparison: "Prove thy servants, I beseech thee, ten days... and as thou seest, deal with thy servants" (Daniel 1:12-13) — which is precisely what the fuller lab panel is: measurement in place of assertion, the Berean rule applied to the body ("Prove all things; hold fast that which is good," 1 Thessalonians 5:21). And the guardrails hold with equal force: eliminations are stewardship for a season and a body, never sanctity — "Let no man therefore judge you in meat, or in drink" (Colossians 2:16); "every creature of God is good, and nothing to be refused, if it be received with thanksgiving" (1 Timothy 4:4); "the kingdom of God is not meat and drink; but righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Ghost" (Romans 14:17). Strength itself is commended in the virtuous woman — "She girdeth her loins with strength, and strengtheneth her arms" (Proverbs 31:17) — and her crown is not follicular: hair is a true glory (1 Corinthians 11:15) and its loss a true grief, worthy of tears and of treatment; yet the adorning of great price is "the hidden man of the heart" (1 Peter 3:4), and "a woman that feareth the LORD, she shall be praised" (Proverbs 31:30). Tend the temple; test the counsel; keep the crown where it cannot fall.',
    },
